# MESH — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is the template
# and must never hold a real secret.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Everything prefixed VITE_ is read at BUILD time and ends up in the JavaScript
# the browser downloads. That is not a leak for the values below — they are
# public identifiers — but it does mean a real secret must never carry that
# prefix, and that changing one requires a rebuild, not just a restart.


# ─── Front end ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Reown project id, from https://dashboard.reown.com → your project → Project ID.
# Enables WalletConnect, so people can connect a wallet on their phone by
# scanning a QR code. Leave it empty and MESH offers browser wallets only, and
# says so in the picker rather than showing a button that cannot work.
#
# Add every domain you deploy to under the project's Allowed Domains, including
# the Netlify URL, or wallets will show the connection as unverified.
VITE_REOWN_PROJECT_ID=

# Origin of the MESH API, e.g. https://mesh-api.onrender.com
#
# Leave EMPTY when the API and the web app share a host — including local
# development, where the dev server proxies /api and /ws for you.
#
# Set it when they are apart, which is the case on a static host like Netlify:
# the API keeps a SQLite database and a WebSocket open, so it cannot run there
# and lives somewhere that can hold those. No trailing slash.
VITE_API_URL=

# The app's canonical URL, e.g. https://meshsocial.com — declared to wallets
# when connecting, and shown by some of them during the prompt.
#
# Leave EMPTY to use whatever origin the page is served from, which is right
# almost always and keeps preview deploys honest about where they are. Set it
# only to a domain registered under Project Domains on your Reown project.
#
# Note this is a claim the app makes, not a proof: it does not by itself make a
# wallet trust the domain. Verification happens on Reown's side.
VITE_APP_URL=

# The chain is read through Robinhood Chain's own published RPC. There is no
# key to configure and nothing to set here.

# Gateway for displaying ipfs:// images, with a trailing slash. Optional.
# A gateway only reads — content is addressed by its own hash, so a different
# gateway returns the same bytes or nothing, never different ones.
# VITE_IPFS_GATEWAY=https://gateway.pinata.cloud/ipfs/


# ─── API server ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by `server/index.js` at runtime. These are NOT sent to the browser.

# Where the API listens. Most hosts set PORT themselves — read that first.
# MESH_API_PORT=8787
# MESH_API_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Path to the SQLite file. The default lives inside the checkout, which most
# hosts wipe on every deploy — so on a real deployment point this at a mounted
# persistent disk instead. Note that free tiers usually do not offer one
# (Render's does not), and without a disk every account is lost on each deploy.
# MESH_DB=/data/mesh.db

# Browsers that may call this API cross-origin, comma separated, no trailing
# slash. Required when VITE_API_URL is set; leave empty for same-origin.
# Include the Netlify production URL and any preview domain you actually use.
# MESH_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-site.netlify.app

# Pinata JWT, from https://app.pinata.cloud → API Keys → New Key.
#
# A CREDENTIAL, and the reason image uploads go through this server at all:
# it can upload anything to the account that owns it. It has NO VITE_ prefix on
# purpose — that prefix would compile it into the JavaScript every visitor
# downloads. Set it in the host's environment settings, never in the repo.
#
# Leave it unset and MESH keeps working: the create form says uploads are
# unavailable and offers the paste-a-URI field instead.
# PINATA_JWT=
